Subject: [PATCH] igc: disable ASPM L1 on ASUS I226-V rev 6 controllers

An ASUS ROG STRIX X870-A GAMING WIFI with I226-V revision 06,
subsystem 1043:8867 and NVM 2023:889d repeatedly loses its PCIe link
on Nobara 7.2.4. The driver detaches the interface after an MMIO read
fails, logging "PCIe link lost, device now detached" followed by
"Failed to read reg 0xc030!". Rebooting restores connectivity.

A report for an ASUS ProArt X870E on Linux 6.12.41 identifies the same
controller revision, subsystem and NVM. Intel attributed the reported
behavior to an unexpected PCIe link state change and recommended
motherboard-vendor investigation. This is not established as a 7.2
regression, and this downstream mitigation still needs hardware soak
testing.

The affected system has ASPM L1 enabled despite the existing I226 L1.2
workaround. Extend that workaround to disable L1 on the reported ASUS
revision and subsystem only. The PCI core also disables dependent L1
substates. Preserve the existing L1.2 mask for all other I226 hardware.

Use the same mask during probe, resume and PCI error recovery, keeping
the locked PCI API in the recovery path. This does not change global
PCIe policy, runtime PM, or the Intel Bluetooth sleep patches.

Link: https://lists.openwall.net/netdev/2025/09/01/324
Link: https://lists.openwall.net/netdev/2025/09/05/17
Link: https://lists.openwall.net/netdev/2025/09/07/41
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/intel/igc/igc_main.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/intel/igc/igc_main.c
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/intel/igc/igc_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/intel/igc/igc_main.c
@@ -7105,6 +7105,24 @@
 	return HRTIMER_NORESTART;
 }
 
+static int igc_aspm_disable_mask(const struct igc_hw *hw)
+{
+	/* Disable ASPM L1.2 on I226 devices to avoid packet loss. */
+	int state = PCIE_LINK_STATE_L1_2;
+
+	/*
+	 * ASUS I226-V rev 6 controllers can lose their PCIe link with L1
+	 * enabled, even when L1.2 is disabled. Keep the workaround scoped
+	 * to the reported hardware; the PCI core also disables L1 substates.
+	 */
+	if (hw->device_id == IGC_DEV_ID_I226_V && hw->revision_id == 0x06 &&
+	    hw->subsystem_vendor_id == PCI_VENDOR_ID_ASUSTEK &&
+	    hw->subsystem_device_id == 0x8867)
+		state |= PCIE_LINK_STATE_L1;
+
+	return state;
+}
+
 /**
  * igc_probe - Device Initialization Routine
  * @pdev: PCI device information struct
@@ -7171,9 +7189,9 @@
 	hw->subsystem_vendor_id = pdev->subsystem_vendor;
 	hw->subsystem_device_id = pdev->subsystem_device;
 
-	/* Disable ASPM L1.2 on I226 devices to avoid packet loss */
+	/* Apply the I226 ASPM workarounds before saving PCI state. */
 	if (igc_is_device_id_i226(hw))
-		pci_disable_link_state(pdev, PCIE_LINK_STATE_L1_2);
+		pci_disable_link_state(pdev, igc_aspm_disable_mask(hw));
 
 	err = pci_save_state(pdev);
 	if (err)
@@ -7559,7 +7577,7 @@
 	pci_enable_wake(pdev, PCI_D3cold, 0);
 
 	if (igc_is_device_id_i226(hw))
-		pci_disable_link_state(pdev, PCIE_LINK_STATE_L1_2);
+		pci_disable_link_state(pdev, igc_aspm_disable_mask(hw));
 
 	if (igc_init_interrupt_scheme(adapter, true)) {
 		netdev_err(netdev, "Unable to allocate memory for queues\n");
@@ -7688,7 +7706,7 @@
 		pci_enable_wake(pdev, PCI_D3cold, 0);
 
 		if (igc_is_device_id_i226(hw))
-			pci_disable_link_state_locked(pdev, PCIE_LINK_STATE_L1_2);
+			pci_disable_link_state_locked(pdev, igc_aspm_disable_mask(hw));
 
 		/* In case of PCI error, adapter loses its HW address
 		 * so we should re-assign it here.
